Review of extended compiler and initial loader (2026-11-15)

Observed executable checks against build/zero:
- All five original hosted execution tests pass exact output/status.
- All 24 original invalid inputs still reject with status1, empty stdout.
- New string literal with load8/load16/load32 yields expected ABC output.
- reserve(65536) succeeds in a hosted program.

PE inspection: build/probe.efi is validly recognized by objdump as PE32+
AMD64, EFI application subsystem10, entry RVA0x1000, 512-byte headers,
4096 section alignment, .text RX, .reloc read/discardable, no imports,
no-op relocation block with two ABSOLUTE entries. Header/section sizes
and relocation-directory offsets are internally consistent.

Code generation static review:
- entry captures RCX/RDX into R14/R15 before prologue clobbers RCX.
- call consumes fn+5 arguments in correct order into RAX,RCX,RDX,R8,R9,R10.
- firmware call stack is aligned16 and has32-byte shadow space, arg5+32.
- R12 saves RSP across call; conforming Microsoft ABI firmware preserves it.
- R13 arena pointer and R14/R15 entry arguments are nonvolatile as required.
- expression temporaries remain below the fixed arena; nested foreign calls
  restore their caller's evaluation-stack pointer after returning.
- enter consumes entry,arg0,stack_top; sets RCX=arg0; disables interrupts;
  aligns new stack16 and jumps. RDX still contains stack_top at kernel entry,
  so arg1 in that kernel is stack_top unless documented otherwise.

Small documentation/alignment point:
reserve rounds lengths16, but its base follows initial RSP. At standard UEFI
function entry, RSP is8 modulo16, so returned pointers remain8 modulo16.
These are valid8-byte-aligned UEFI struct/GUID buffers, but do not promise
16-byte-aligned allocation unless the compiler prologue additionally aligns
RSP before establishing its frame/arena. ELF entry and enter() alignment differ.

Initial loader source interface review:
LoadedImage/SimpleFS/File offsets and GUID encodings match official specs.
Kernel allocated as EfiLoaderCode; dedicated kernel stack allocated LoaderData.
File read loop honors returned byte count; data-map buffer is allocated before
final GetMemoryMap and ExitBootServices. Exit invalid-key retry uses a fresh map
and no forbidden console/protocol calls after the first attempt.
The fixed64KiB map capacity fails explicitly if insufficient; it does not resize.
The loader's direct UART writes lack transmitter-ready polling and can lose
some diagnostic bytes on slower hardware; this does not alter boot logic.

Not claimed by this review: firmware execution of PE, completion of boot,
execution of privileged intrinsics, or physical hardware behavior. Those need
parent's full-machine tests. No source modifications were made by reviewer.
